WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.628
A common SOC 2 mistake is treating the audit as a one-time sprint.

2
00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:12.122
Teams scramble, collect screenshots, chase owners, clean up policies, and
hope the report works out.

3
00:00:12.122 --> 00:00:19.914
That approach may sometimes get through a narrow milestone, but it does
not create a sustainable control program.

4
00:00:19.914 --> 00:00:27.274
SOC 2 readiness is stronger when evidence is produced by normal
operations. Unclear scope is another pitfall.

5
00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:33.767
If teams do not know which systems, services, environments, people, and
controls are in scope,

6
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:37.664
they collect the wrong evidence or miss important evidence.

7
00:00:37.664 --> 00:00:41.127
Customers may also misunderstand what the report covers.

8
00:00:41.127 --> 00:00:48.053
Scope should be clear enough that control owners know whether their work
matters to the report.

9
00:00:48.053 --> 00:00:50.651
Lack of ownership creates failure points.

10
00:00:50.651 --> 00:00:56.279
A control with no owner often becomes visible only when evidence is
missing.

11
00:00:56.279 --> 00:01:03.205
Each control should have a responsible owner, an operating frequency, an
evidence location, an exception process,

12
00:01:03.205 --> 00:01:05.005
and a backup plan.

13
00:01:05.005 --> 00:01:11.932
If only one person knows how to operate a control, the organization has a
continuity risk.

14
00:01:11.932 --> 00:01:14.530
Manual evidence chaos does not scale.

15
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:21.023
If every evidence request requires a custom search through chats,
screenshots, spreadsheets, and private notes,

16
00:01:21.023 --> 00:01:23.188
the program will be fragile.

17
00:01:23.188 --> 00:01:30.980
Sustainable programs build evidence into ticketing, identity systems, HR
workflows, vendor review tools, incident response records, monitoring
processes,

18
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.145
backup testing, and change management.

19
00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:40.504
The best evidence is often created naturally when work is done correctly.
Exceptions are not the enemy.

20
00:01:40.504 --> 00:01:42.669
Ignoring exceptions is the problem.

21
00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.267
Access reviews may find inappropriate access.

22
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:50.029
Change tickets may miss a field. A backup test may fail.

23
00:01:50.029 --> 00:01:52.626
A vendor review may be late.

24
00:01:52.626 --> 00:01:57.821
These should be tracked, evaluated, remediated, and used to improve the
process.

25
00:01:57.821 --> 00:02:03.449
Hiding exceptions or pretending they did not happen damages trust and
prevents learning.

26
00:02:03.449 --> 00:02:09.077
Sustainable SOC 2 operations continue when the auditor is not actively
asking questions.

27
00:02:09.077 --> 00:02:16.003
Teams follow the process because it supports reliability, security, and
customer trust. Policies match actual practice.

28
00:02:16.003 --> 00:02:21.198
Controls have owners. Evidence is retained. Exceptions are visible.
Remediation is tracked.

29
00:02:21.198 --> 00:02:27.624
The report then becomes a reflection of operating discipline rather than a
stressful reconstruction project.
